msgid "What is the OpenJ9 Shared Classes Cache?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Slow startup times have long been a challenge for Java applications. Project "
"Leyden addressed this for JVMs like Temurin 25+, which are based on HotSpot, "
"but what about users of JVMs based on IBM OpenJ9 like Semeru?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In our https://quarkus.io/blog/leyden-2/[previous post], we described how we "
"integrated Project Leyden into Quarkus, bringing JVM startup way down."
msgstr ""

msgid "But not everyone runs HotSpot-based JVMs."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Many teams, especially in enterprise environments, run https://www.ibm.com/"
"semeru-runtimes[IBM Semeru Runtimes], IBM's production Java runtime built on "
"the https://eclipse.dev/openj9/[Eclipse OpenJ9] JVM.\n"
"These teams deserve the similar startup improvements, with the same ease of "
"use."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"That's what we built. Starting with Quarkus 3.35, the same `quarkus.package."
"jar.aot.enabled=true` flag that activates Leyden on OpenJDK now "
"automatically generates an OpenJ9 Shared Classes Cache on IBM Semeru."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"No code changes. No additional configuration. Quarkus detects the JVM and "
"does the right thing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Eclipse OpenJ9 JVM has long offered a feature called https://eclipse.dev/"
"openj9/docs/shrc/[Shared Classes Cache (SCC)].\n"
"The concept is similar to what Project Leyden does for HotSpot, but it "
"predates Leyden by many years and goes further in some respects."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At its core, the SCC is a region of shared memory — either a memory-mapped "
"file (persistent) or shared memory segment (non-persistent) — that stores "
"data the JVM would otherwise have to recompute on every startup.\n"
"When a class is loaded for the first time, OpenJ9 automatically stores it in "
"the cache.\n"
"On subsequent startups, the JVM finds it there and skips parsing, "
"verification, and loading from disk entirely."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"But class data is only the beginning. The SCC stores three categories of "
"data:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Class metadata"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Parsed and verified class structures, ready to use without re-reading JAR "
"files."
msgstr ""

msgid "AOT-compiled native code"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The OpenJ9 AOT compiler dynamically compiles Java methods into native code "
"at runtime and stores them in the cache. On subsequent runs, these pre-"
"compiled methods execute immediately instead of being interpreted. The VM "
"https://eclipse.dev/openj9/docs/aot/[automatically selects] which methods to "
"AOT-compile using heuristics that identify the startup phase of large "
"applications — no manual configuration needed."
msgstr ""

msgid "JIT profiling data and compilation hints"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The JIT compiler stores profiling information and optimization hints in the "
"cache, allowing subsequent JVM instances to make better compilation "
"decisions from the start. When a cached AOT method runs, the JIT compiler "
"can further optimize it based on actual runtime behavior, giving you the "
"best of both worlds."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The result: significantly faster startup, lower memory overhead during class "
"loading, and a warmer JIT from the very first request."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Unlike other class data sharing implementations, the OpenJ9 SCC is fully "
"dynamic: it is populated transparently as your application runs, without "
"requiring a separate offline step to enumerate classes.\n"
"The cache is also designed for multi-JVM environments — multiple JVM "
"instances can share the same cache simultaneously, reducing the aggregate "
"memory footprint when running several Java applications on the same host."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are familiar with how Leyden works, much of this will sound familiar. "
"The key difference is that SCC is specific to the OpenJ9 JVM and has been "
"production-hardened over many years, while Leyden is a newer effort within "
"OpenJDK targeting HotSpot."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using it"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you already have `quarkus.package.jar.aot.enabled=true` in your build, "
"you don't need to change anything. Just build with a Semeru JDK:"
msgstr ""

msgid "That's it. The build:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Packages your application with the `aot-jar` format."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Starts the application with `-Xshareclasses:name=quarkus-app,cacheDir=app-"
"scc`, which tells OpenJ9 to populate the cache as the application runs."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your `@QuarkusIntegrationTest` tests run against it, exercising your "
"endpoints and features. During this time, OpenJ9 is continuously populating "
"the cache with class data, AOT-compiled methods, and JIT profiling hints."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When the tests finish and the application shuts down, the `app-scc/` "
"directory contains a fully populated Shared Classes Cache."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Once that is done, verify that your logs contain `Detected IBM Semeru "
"Runtime - using Shared Classes Cache`"
msgstr ""

msgid "To run the application with the cache:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `readonly` flag prevents the cache from being modified at runtime.\n"
"This is the recommended setting for production: the cache is an artifact of "
"the build, not something that should drift in production."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The generated `app-scc/` directory must be deployed alongside your JAR:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Shared Classes Cache directory. Its contents are opaque to the user — "
"OpenJ9 manages the internal structure."
msgstr ""

msgid "Customizing the training run"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need to pass additional JVM flags during the training run (for "
"example, to increase heap size or enable specific JVM features), you can use:"
""
msgstr ""

msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the cache doesn't seem to improve startup, first verify it is actually "
"being used.\n"
"Enable verbose output by replacing `readonly` with `readonly,verboseIO`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This prints detailed information about each class loaded from the cache "
"versus the filesystem."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Also make sure you are using the exact same Semeru JVM version to run the "
"application as was used to build the cache.\n"
"OpenJ9 uses internal generation numbers to detect incompatible caches; a "
"version mismatch will cause the cache to be ignored."
msgstr ""

msgid "How auto-detection works"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When `quarkus.package.jar.aot.enabled=true` is set and no explicit type is "
"configured, Quarkus picks the best strategy for the JVM it detects at build "
"time:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Build JVM"
msgstr ""

msgid "Strategy"
msgstr ""

msgid "Output"
msgstr ""

msgid "IBM Semeru"
msgstr ""

msgid "Shared Classes Cache (`-Xshareclasses`)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`app-scc/` directory"
msgstr ""

msgid "HotSpot (25+)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Leyden AOT (`-XX:AOTCache`)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`app.aot` file"
msgstr ""

msgid "Older HotSpot"
msgstr ""

msgid "AppCDS (`-XX:SharedArchiveFile`)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`app-cds.jsa` file"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The detection checks `java.runtime.name` for the string `semeru`. If it "
"matches, the SCC path is chosen. Otherwise, Quarkus checks the Java version: "
"25 or higher selects Leyden, anything older falls back to AppCDS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need to override the auto-detection (for example, to force AppCDS on "
"Semeru for comparison), you can set the type explicitly:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Valid values are `AUTO` (the default), `AOT`, `AppCDS`, and `SCC`."
msgstr ""

msgid "One flag, every JVM"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is the design decision we are most proud of in this work. Your build "
"configuration, CI pipeline, and Dockerfile don't need to know which JVM will "
"run the application. Set `quarkus.package.jar.aot.enabled=true`, and the "
"build adapts."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This matters in practice. Teams that standardize on Semeru for some services "
"and HotSpot for others can use the same Quarkus build configuration "
"everywhere. Switching JVMs doesn't require touching `application.properties`."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It also means that the Quarkus documentation, guides, and examples work the "
"same way regardless of the JVM.\n"
"When we write \"enable AOT for faster startup,\" that statement is true "
"whether you run HotSpot, Semeru, or an older JDK."
msgstr ""

